From 5097dec132bc0f03350f02a1a1dfd52c00b775c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:54:04 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] ingress: bind the crossing to the delivered manifest's
 exact payload

`receive` reconstructed every boundary receipt against the crossing's own
`payload_hash` but never recomputed that digest from the manifest it was
handed. A crossing whose observed effect (`application_result.echo.manifest`)
was changed and whose `manifest_sha256` was zeroed, with `payload_hash` kept,
still reconstructed all five closures and was admitted (independent review of
StegVerse-Labs/.github#3375; #3369 requires exact-byte payload identity and
binding, and its test matrix requires payload tamper to be DENY/FAIL_CLOSED).

The payload a crossing carries is now built by one function,
`sdk_manifest_crossing.crossing_payload` (the existing definition, moved, not
changed), and `payload_hash` stays `sha` of it: canonical JSON, sorted keys,
no whitespace -- the same bytes `process_boundary.py` hashes into every
receipt. After the chain reconstructs, `receive` recomputes that payload from
the delivered manifest and its own declared destination and refuses, FAIL_CLOSED
under CROSSING_PAYLOAD_RECOMPUTES_FROM_THE_DELIVERED_MANIFEST with failure code
CROSSING_PAYLOAD_NOT_BOUND_TO_THE_DELIVERED_MANIFEST and the six non-ALLOW
fields, when `manifest_sha256`, `payload_hash` or an echoed effect is not that
payload. The refusal is recorded at both levels through `_refused`.

Three existing test stubs modelled crossings bound to nothing (a placeholder
`payload_hash`, no `manifest_sha256`, manifests declaring no egress). They now
build their crossing from the delivered manifest with the crossing's own
payload definition; no assertion changed. New regression tests drive the real
crossing, boundary subprocess and closure reconstruction:
tests/test_organization_ingress_payload_binding.py. .stegverse/test-sets.json
regenerated.

Refs StegVerse-Labs/.github#3369, StegVerse-Labs/.github#3375

